        "title": "Salary Components Breakdown",
        "content": "Aao ab salary slip ke ek-ek component ki anatomically post-mortem karte hain. Ek-ek allowance ka business rationale, tax impact, aur ideal ratio samjhoge tabhi tax bachaa paoge aur HR se next job Switch par smartly negotiate kar paoge.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.1 Basic Salary — The Anchor of Your Paycheck\n\n**Basic Salary** tumhari total earnings ka foundational core hota hai. Structure mein baki saare components (HRA, EPF, Gratuity, Leave Encashment, Overtime) Basic Salary ke percentage (%) ke roop mein calculate kiye jaate hain.\n\n#### Golden Rule of Basic Salary Ratio:\n> **Ideal Basic Salary = 40% to 50% of total CTC.**\n\nWhy is this ratio critical?\n\n#### Scenario Analysis: Comparative Impact of Basic Salary Ratios on a ₹10 LPA CTC\n\nLet's compare three different corporate pay structures for the exact same **₹10,00,000 CTC package**:\n\n#### Table 16.3: Impact of Basic Salary Percentage on Take-Home & Benefits (₹10 LPA CTC)\n\n| Parameter | Low Basic Structure (30% Basic) | Ideal Basic Structure (50% Basic) | High Basic Structure (70% Basic) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Annual Basic Salary** | ₹3,00,000 (₹25,000/mo) | ₹5,00,000 (₹41,667/mo) | ₹7,00,000 (₹58,333/mo) |\n| **Annual HRA (50% of Basic)** | ₹1,50,000 (₹12,500/mo) | ₹2,50,000 (₹20,833/mo) | ₹3,50,000 (₹29,167/mo) |\n| **Employer EPF (12% of Basic)**| ₹36,000 (₹3,000/mo) | ₹60,000 (₹5,000/mo) | ₹84,000 (₹7,000/mo) |\n| **Gratuity Pool (4.81% Basic)**| ₹14,430 (₹1,203/mo) | ₹24,050 (₹2,004/mo) | ₹33,670 (₹2,806/mo) |\n| **Special Allowance (Balance)**| ₹4,99,570 (₹41,631/mo) | ₹1,65,950 (₹13,829/mo) | ₹0 |\n| **Monthly In-Hand (Approx)** | **~₹68,500 / month** | **~₹64,200 / month** | **~₹59,800 / month** |\n| **Max HRA Tax Exemption** | Low Exemption Cap (₹1.5L) | High Exemption Cap (₹2.5L)| Very High Cap (₹3.5L) |\n| **Retirement Savings Rate** | Slow Wealth Building | Balanced Wealth Building | Aggressive Wealth Building |\n\n#### Insights from Table 16.3:\n- **Low Basic (30%):** Monthly in-hand cash highest dikhta hai (~₹68,500), lekin retirement fund (EPF) speed weak ho jaati hai aur HRA tax exemption cap reduce ho jaata hai.\n- **High Basic (70%):** Retirement wealth (EPF) fast build hota hai, lekin immediate monthly take-home cash drops to ~₹59,800 due to heavy 12% PF deductions on ₹7 Lakhs.\n- **Ideal Basic (50%):** Perfect equilibrium between immediate monthly cash flow, optimal tax shelter (HRA), and statutory retiral safety.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.2 House Rent Allowance (HRA) Calculation & Exemption Rules\n\nAgar tum job ke liye kisi dusre city mein shift hue ho (e.g., Bangalore, Gurgaon, Pune, Mumbai, Hyderabad) aur rent par reh rahe ho, toh **HRA tumhara sabse bada tax-saving shield hai under Old Tax Regime**.\n\n*(Note: Direct Tax basics aur tax regime differences detail mein samajhne ke liye **Module 10: Tax Basics for Students** zaroor review karo).*\n\nUnder Section 10(13A) of Income Tax Act, HRA Exemption ki calculation niche diye gaye **3 components mein se Minimum (Sabse Kam Amount)** ke barabar hoti hai:\n\n#### The 3-Rule HRA Exemption Formula:\n\n```\nHRA Exemption Amount = MINIMUM OF THE FOLLOWING THREE VALUES:\n1. Actual HRA Amount Received from Employer\n2. Rent Paid minus 10% of Basic Salary (Rent Paid - [10% x Basic Salary])\n3. Metro City Limit: 50% of Basic Salary  (For Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai)\n   NON-Metro City Limit: 40% of Basic Salary (For Bangalore, Hyderabad, Pune, Gurgaon, etc.)\n```\n\n> **Crucial Clarification:** Income Tax Act ke statutory definitions mein, India ke major tech hubs like **Bangalore, Hyderabad, Pune, Noida, Gurgaon Metro cities nahi maane jaate HRA calculation ke liye!** Unhe 40% non-metro category mein count kiya jaata hai. Metro category strictly *Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai* ke municipal core areas par apply hoti hai.\n\n---\n\n#### Real Calculation Walkthrough: 3 Rent Scenarios",
